## Three Resources

**1. "Show Your Work" — Austin Kleon**
Short, visual, no fluff. The core argument: you don't have to be a genius, you just have to be a contributor. The chapter on sharing something small every day has genuinely changed how a lot of creators think about visibility.

**2. The Zettelkasten Method (Nick Milo's intro on YouTube)**
If you make anything idea-driven — writing, video, design — you eventually hit the problem of losing good thoughts. The Zettelkasten is a note-linking system that turns your scattered ideas into a searchable creative archive. Takes an hour to understand, pays dividends for years.

**3. Beehiiv (free plan)**
If you've been thinking about a newsletter — which, given that you're reading this one, you might be — Beehiiv's free plan is currently the cleanest entry point. No subscriber cap on the free tier (up to 2,500), no Beehiiv branding on your emails, and the editor is actually pleasant to use.

